window.WIKI_ENTRY={"id": "concept-105", "name_zh": "或然事件", "name_en": "probable events", "tagline": "赛斯给「没发生的事」安排了一个正式位置：或然事件照样是事件，你没感知到不等于它不存在——你的日常实相只是那一大片里被挑中、被聚焦过的一条线。", "explanation": "或然事件（probable events）是赛斯给「没有被你感知到的事件」安排的正式位置。他的说法不是「本来可能发生但没发生」：可能性事件的场域本身就是一个实相，行动就是行动，不管有没有人经验到它。你的意识受物质时间的限制，一次只能聚焦一条线，于是那个场域里被挑中的那一个成为你的物质实相，没被挑走的那些由你自己的其他部分——包括梦里的你——照样去经验。这套说法在早期课第 5 册成形（第 226 至 234 节，1966 年，事件X的推演、梦苹果、其他维度里变得「真实」），在《个人实相的本质》第 653 至 657 节变成机制与日常用法（信念过滤、神经系统、入室盗窃的例子、换掉一段往事），到《未知的实相》《心灵的本质》第 694 至 789 节被放大成集体与宇宙尺度（历史与文化被可能事件染色、星球与火箭船的比喻、每个细胞在睡眠里计算可能事件的影响），最后在第 857、905、931 节和《健康之道》第 8 节收束为宇宙的动力与跨生的储存所。对新手最实际的是两条：一，你看到的世界只是被聚焦过的一串，不是唯一存在的那串；二，信念与强度决定了哪些可能性落地，所以「没发生的事」也还在你的实相里工作。这一页只讲事件本身；去经验这些事件的另一部分自己，写在「或然的自己」那一页；更宽的那张网叫「可能性」，是单独的一页。", "definitions": [{"zh": "因为不管你们有没有感知到它，行动就是行动，而可能性事件也是一样，不管你有没有感知到它，它就是事件。", "en": "For action is action whether or not you perceive it, and probable events are events whether or not you perceive them as such.", "source": "Session 231"}, {"zh": "可能性事件的场域是以一个实相存在的。自己从中选择那些应该会变成一部分物质实相的行动。", "en": "The field of probable events exists as a reality. From it the self chooses those actions which shall become a part of physical reality.", "source": "Session 234"}, {"zh": "以你们的话来谈，可能的事件被带入现实，是借由身体的神经系统而透过意志或有意识信念的某种强度来达到的。", "en": "In your terms probable events are brought into actuality by utilizing the body’s nerve structure through certain intensities of will or conscious belief.", "source": "Session 653"}], "blocks": [{"id": "B01", "title": "或然事件是什么：没被感知，也照样是事件", "note": "这一页的定则藏在第 231 节的一句话里：行动就是行动，不管你有没有感知到它；可能性事件也是一样，不管你有没有感知到它，它就是事件。第 226 节先交代前情——透过预知或洞察力看到的那个未来事件可能发生也可能不发生，因为你看到的是可能性，而它还得落进你们的时间系统。第 234 节给出整张图的形状：可能性事件的场域是以一个实相存在的，自己从中选择那些应该会变成物质实相一部分的行动，自由意志到这里才进场（赛斯自己说这是个双关，还强调那是句好双关）。第 654 节替新手把理解方式说定：把它们想成潜伏的将来事件，最讲得通。第 787 节收住：你生活中所有的可能事件在连着梦境的某个层面同时存在着，只因你的活动必须合入时空架构，实质发生的只是其中最小的一部分。读这一页最该抓住的一点是：或然事件不是「没发生的事」，它是一种没被你聚焦的存在方式，而你称之为现实的那串，只是它的一条线。", "src": "第 226、231、234、654、787 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 226 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 231 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 234 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 654 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 787 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "行动不需要观众", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0011", "zh": "这里有很多含义。现在你们可能会了解为什么在我介绍倒置时间系统之前，我要先给你们有关行动本质的资料。因为不管你们有没有感知到它，行动就是行动，而可能性事件也是一样，不管你有没有感知到它，它就是事件。", "en": "There are many implications here, and now perhaps you can understand why I gave you the information on the nature of action before I introduced you to the inverted time system. For action is action whether or not you perceive it, and probable events are events whether or not you perceive them as such.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第231节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "231", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0001", "zh": "我希望把这些重点说得很清楚。现在。一个透过预知力或洞察力所预见的事件，一个未来的事件，可能会或可能不会在你们所知道的时间里真实的发生。因为你们看到的是可能性，而这个可能的事件，在你们的时间系统里，可能会或可能不会发生。", "en": "I would like these points made most clearly. Now. An event foreseen through precognition or clairvoyance, a future event, may or may not actually occur within time as you know it. For you are seeing into probabilities, and the probable event may or may not occur, within your time system.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第226节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "226", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0002", "zh": "感知者的整个心理组成是完全不同的，没有一个事件是在所有可能的事件之外，但所有数学上可能事件的经验，能发生在任何你所知道的特定时间内任何特定的个人身上。", "en": "The whole psychological formation of the perceiver is entirely different, and there is no one event out of all probable events, but there is experience of all the mathematically probable events that could happen to any given individual, within any given amount of time as you know it.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第226节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "226", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "一个已经存在的场域，等你从中挑", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0023", "zh": "可能性事件的场域是以一个实相存在的。自己从中选择那些应该会变成一部分物质实相的行动。这里的确关乎自由意志（或：这里自由意志的确进入物质）。这是一个双关语。如果它被遗漏了，我会很失望的。它是很好的一句双关语。", "en": "The field of probable events exists as a reality. From it the self chooses those actions which shall become a part of physical reality. Here indeed free will enters into the matter. This is a pun. I would be disappointed if it were missed. It is a good one.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第234节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "234", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0037", "zh": "以你们的话实际来说，当你把可能的事件想成潜在的将来事件，它们似乎比较合理。", "en": "In your terms, practically speaking, probable events seem to make more sense when you think of them as latent future ones.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第654节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "654", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0066", "zh": "现在：在与梦境相连的某个层面，所有你生活中的可能事件都同时存在着。既然你的活动实质上必须合入一个时空的架构，那些可能事件只有极小的一部分会实质地发生。", "en": "Now: All of the probable events of your life exist at once, at certain levels that are connected to the dream state. Since your activities physically must be fitted into a space-time framework, only a minimum of those probable events will physically occur.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第787节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "787", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B02", "title": "事件X：为什么你只看得见一条线", "note": "第 227 节那段推演最好懂，也最能说明赛斯为什么要用事件X这种讲法：事件X只是无数或然事件里唯一一个被有意识的自己经验到的；在它被经验之前，它跟其他可能事件没有基本样子上的不同；如果你选择去感知的是事件Y，那么Y就是实相，X就仍不是真的。结论很硬——让X变「真」的不是X本身，是被感知。同节接着解释为什么一次只能挑一个：物质时间这样运作，肉体有机体在自己的框架下没有时间去经验超过一个可能事件，无法同时聚焦在两件事上，何况每一个物质事件事实上已经是许多小事件的完形。于是一部分自己就转进所有其他本来很容易被自我经验的可能事件；第 227 节末尾用握手作比，你不必逐个去感知组成握手的百万个小动作，你以它们完成的方式感知它们，而那部分自己经验这些可能事件的速度，跟你潜意识感知这些小动作一样快。第 231 节把同一个事件讲成三面：被自我经验时你叫它真实物质事件，被自己的其他层面经验时自我毫不知情，而这些经验的方式是任何一个行动都被带着各种各样、几乎无止尽的变化推到底。", "src": "第 227、231 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 227 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 231 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "被感知的那一个才「变成真的」", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0003", "zh": "现在这个事件X，是无数可能性事件中唯一一个有意识的自己会经验到的。因为它的目的，这个有意识自己选择了这一个特定的事件X。但再次的，直到有意识的自己经验它之前，这个事件X是许多可能事件的其中一个，与其他的可能事件并没有基本样子上的不同。只有当它被我们有意识的自己或这个有意识的自己经验之后，它就变得真实，以及有别于其他的可能事件。", "en": "Now this event X is only one of a literally numberless amount of probable events which the conscious self could experience. For its purposes however the conscious self chooses this particular event X. But again, this event X, until the conscious self experiences it, is only one of many other probable events, different in no basic manner from the others. It becomes real, actual and different from those other probable events, only when it is experienced by our conscious self, or by this conscious self.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第227节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "227", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0004", "zh": "然后用你们的话来说，这个事件变成真的，只有当它被我们有意识的自己经验或感知到之后。不过，那所有这些其他的可能事件怎样了呢？在它们和事件X之间唯一的差别是，事件X被我们有意识的个体感知或经验到了。换句话说，如果没有对事件X的感知，它就像其他所有没有被感知到的可能事件一样，或是有效的，或无效的，或是真的，或不是真的。", "en": "This event X becomes real then, in your terms, only when it is experienced or perceived by our conscious self. What about all of these other probable events, however? The only difference between them and event X is that event X was perceived and experienced by our conscious individual. In other words, were it not for this perception of the event X, it would still be as valid, or as invalid, as real or as unreal, as all the other probable events that were not perceived.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第227节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "227", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0005", "zh": "这引导我们到一个明显的结论：如果事件X没有被感知到，它就仍然只是一个可能性。同样的，如果我们选择去感知和经验，比如说，事件Y的话，那么事件Y就会是实相，而事件X就仍不是真的。", "en": "This leads us then to an obvious conclusion: if event X were not perceived it would still be a probability only. By the same token, if our individual chose to perceive and experience, say, event Y, then event Y would be the reality, and event X would still be unreal. As I have explained the ego to you, within your system it can only perceive in terms of continuity, in a straight-line fashion so to speak, one event after the other. It can only choose to experience one event out of all the probable events at a time. The ego is however the only portion of the self that is, in the main, limited to follow experience along these lines.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第227节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "227", "only_en": true}]}, {"label": "物质有机体一次只能聚焦一件事", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0006", "zh": "因为你们的物质时间以这样的方式运作，物质有机体在自己的框架下没有时间去经验任何超过一个的可能性事件。它无法同时聚焦在两件事上。不用说，我们把事情相当程度的简单化了，因为每一个物质事件事实上是很多小事件的一个完形。", "en": "Since your physical time operates as it does, the physical organism does not have time within its own framework to experience any more than one probable event. It cannot focus upon two events at once. It goes without saying, again, that we are simplifying matters considerably, since each physical event is actually a gestalt of many small events.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第227节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "227", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0007", "zh": "你能专注且理解的经验包裹确实是由许多小包裹所组成的，但实相的整个包裹事实上比这个要更广大。不过有一部分的自己可以，而且的确能以一种完全不一样的方式来经验着事件，而这部分的自己走向了一条不同的路。当我们个人感知了事件X，可以说这自己的另一部分转入了所有其他可能很容易被自我经验的可能事件。", "en": "The package of experience that you can focus upon and make sense of, is indeed composed of many small packages, but the whole package of reality is actually much larger than this. There is however a portion of the self that can and does experience events in an entirely different fashion, and this portion of the self goes off on a different tangent. For when our individual perceives event X, this other portion of the self branches off, so to speak, into all the other probable events that could have been just as easily experienced by the ego.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第227节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "227", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0008", "zh": "尽管如此，这些动作是存在的。你没有花时间逐一的去感知它们。你以它们完成的方式去感知它们。现在这个自己的其他部分，有意识的，经验着这些可能性，以和你潜意识感知到组成握手的百万个小动作同样快的程度。", "en": "These actions exist nevertheless. It does not take you time to perceive them one by one. You perceive them in their completed fashion. Now this other portion of the self experiences these probable events, consciously, with as much rapidity as you subconsciously perceive the million small actions that make up the handshake.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第227节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "227", "only_en": true}]}, {"label": "同一个事件，自己的其他层在经验它", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0013", "zh": "现在。以可能性事件X来做例子。这个所谓的可能性事件或行动，会被自己的不同部分以它们自己的方式来经验。当它被自我经验的时候，你可称它为一个真实的物质事件。当这个事件被自己的其他层面感知或经验时，自我是不知道的。", "en": "Now. Take for example probable event X. This so-called probable event or action will be experienced by the various portions of the self in their own way. When it is experienced by the ego you call it an actual physical event. When the event is perceived or experienced by other layers of the self, the ego does not know of it.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第231节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "231", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0015", "zh": "这些可能性事件以这样的方式被经验，任何一个特定的或可能性的行动伴随着各种各样、几乎是无止尽的变化。", "en": "The probable events are experienced in such a way that any given action or probable action is followed through in its various and almost infinite varieties.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第231节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "231", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B03", "title": "没被挑走的那些并没有消失", "note": "这一块的用处是把没被选中的那些从垃圾桶里捞回来。第 227 节说得最直白：可能性事件确实和其中一个被选作物质经验的那个事件同样真实，所以它们在其他维度里也变得真实——他还特意给「真实」加上引号。同一节留了一条少有人注意的副作用：一次严重的心理惊吓，或一种深到难以忍受的无奈感，会造成一个短路，使自己的一部分变得觉知、开始经验另一部分所在的实相；那种人正在经验一个可能事件，只是必须在他自己的时间系统里经验。第 232 节的梦苹果是同一个意思：梦见握着苹果，醒来苹果不见了，并不代表苹果不存在，只代表你在醒时状态没觉知到它的实相；你的一部分可能相当深地涉入这些事件，所以你的另一部分也在这里关切着，只是那部分你平常不熟悉。第 567 节把界线整个抽掉：不可能把一个实质事件与所有那些可能事件分开，因为它们全是一个行动的各种面目，事件之间的次元是个幻象，彼此之间总存在内在通道。第 671 节给真假重新划线：梦里经验的可能事件在实相的其他领域相当合法，到你们的世界里就变得不成立，而同类事件一旦实质实现，就成了真的。第 682 节留下这一页口气最大的一句：能够发生在你身上的每一个可能事件，都发生了。", "src": "第 227、232、567、671、682 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 227 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 232 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 567 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 671 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 682 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "换个维度，它照样在发生", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0009", "zh": "当然，想像力能模糊地感知到一些可能性，但物质有机体就连续性来说，只能在物质时间里直接经验其中的一个。不过，可能性确实和它们之间其中一个被选为物质经验的那个事件是同样的真实。因此，这些事件在其他维度变得“真实”，引号。这里有一条副线，有一些有趣的插曲，不全然被了解，当一个严重的心理惊吓，或甚至一种很深的、难以忍受的无奈感发生时，会产生一个短路，可以这么说，因此自己的一部分变得觉知，并开始经验自己另一部分存在的实相。", "en": "The imagination can vaguely perceive, of course, some probabilities, but the physical organism can directly experience but one of these within physical time, and in terms of continuity. The probable events however are precisely as real as that one event which is chosen from them to be a physical experience. And these events therefore become “real”, in quotes, within other dimensions. As a sideline here, there are some interesting episodes, not at all understood, when a severe psychological shock, or even a deep sense of unendurable futility, will cause a short circuit, so to speak, so that one portion of the self becomes aware, and begins to experience reality as it exists for another portion of the self.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第227节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "227", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0010", "zh": "在一些这样的例子当中，个人正经验着一个可能性的事件。但你知道，他必须在他自己的时间系统里经验它。", "en": "In some such cases the individual is experiencing a probable event. But he must experience it, you see, within his own time system.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第227节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "227", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0016", "zh": "可能性以同样的方式存在。它们对你来说不是具体的，就像梦对你来说也不是具体的。例如，你可能梦见握着一个苹果，然后醒来苹果就不见了。这并不代表这个苹果不存在。它只是代表了在醒时的状态，你没有觉知到它的实相。你没有经验它，或感知它。以同样的方式，你没有物质上的感知或经验到可能事件的真实。", "en": "Now, probabilities exist in the same manner. They simply are not concrete for you, as dreams are not concrete for you. You may dream for example of holding an apple, then wake up and the apple is gone. This does not mean that the apple did not exist. It simply means that in the waking state you are not aware of its reality. You do not experience it, or perceive it. In the same manner you do not perceive or experience physically the actuality of probable events.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第232节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "232", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0017", "zh": "现在你的一部分可能相当涉入这些可能性的事件。因此你自己的另一部分也在这里关切着，但那是你平常不熟悉的一部分。", "en": "Now a portion of you may be quite involved in these probable events. Another portion of yourself therefore is concerned here, and a portion with which you are not ordinarily familiar.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第232节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "232", "only_en": true}]}, {"label": "实质事件与可能事件分不开", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0031", "zh": "广义地说，是不可能将一个实质事件与所有那些可能事件分开的，因为这些全是一个行动的各种面目。同理，基本上不可能把你所知的“你”和你不知的可能的你分开。不过，在各个可能事件之间，总是有内在的通道；既然它们全是一个行动在其成形中的表现，那么在这些事件之间的次元就是个幻象。", "en": "In greater terms, it is impossible to separate one physical event from the probable events, for these are all dimensions of one action. It is basically impossible to separate the “you” that you know from the probable you’s of which you are unaware, for the same reasons. There are always inner pathways, however, leading between probable events; since all of them are manifestations of an act in its becoming, then the dimensions between these are illusions.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第567节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "567", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0041", "zh": "在梦里体验的可能事件，在实相的其他领域是相当“合法”的，但是在你们的世界里就变得不成立了，而当同类事件实质地在你们的世界里实现时，就成了真的。", "en": "Probable events, experienced dream-wise, and quite valid in other areas of reality, become, say, false in your world, while the same kind of event, physically actualized, becomes true.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第671节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "671", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0048", "zh": "当然，根本没有一种单线式的发展。首先，如你所知，你的生命是即刻的，虽然实际地你体验到一个由生到死的顺序——如鲁柏在《意识的探险》里的生活范围。每个能够发生在你身上的可能事件都发生了。我给过你母亲的可能存在一两个小小的例子。以具体的说法，想想看从一粒种子生出到世世代代里的后代有多少！", "en": "There is, of course, no single-line kind of development at all. In the first place, as you know, your life is at once, though you experience, practically, a life-to-death sequence — Ruburt’s living area in Adventures.7 Every probable event that could happen to you, happens. I gave you one or two small examples of your mother’s probable existences. Think in physical terms of the generations going out from one seed into the ages.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第682节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "682", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B04", "title": "什么东西把或然事件带进物质：信念、强度、神经系统", "note": "第 653 节那一整段的题目就是这一页的标题：你的日常实相是特定可能事件的表现。机制讲得很具体——头脑是心智的实质副本，灵魂与智力的功能借它和身体相连，非实质根源的事件透过头脑的特性变成实质有效的，这中间是一个明确的过滤与集中作用；实际操作上，你的确是透过有意识的信念形成实相所采取的面貌，那些信念充当过滤与指挥的原动力，把某些非实质的可能事件从其他里面分出来、带进三维现实。同一节后半句对新手最有用：其他可能事件同样可以变成被实质经验的事件，是你对自己的信念形成了自我形象、并定义了对你来说什么可能什么不可能，所以你只挑那些你觉得自己与之相符的。第 789 节把落地的条件说细：要变成实质，或然事件必须落进恰当的时空位置，还必须有心理上的契合，也就是欲望、信念或意图达到某种强度；他特别澄清强度不一定指努力、猛烈的欲望或下决心的有意识意向，而是某些无形质物的集合，精确地向实质活动聚焦。所以一个实质事件在他眼里是一个心理组织——一组被选择的可能事件在侧翼等待实质实现，最后的扳机可以从醒时或梦里来。第 672 节说明醒时的意识心在这条链上干什么活：它被派去估量每日生活的「事实」、形成对实相的信念，这些信念再被用到梦里充当主要的尺度之一，启动某些可能事件而非其他。第 531 节则把整件事安放在睡眠里：意识离开身体，你处置每日经验、把它投射到你以为的将来，从无限的可能事件中选择你愿将它具体化的那些，然后开始把它们带进物质世界的精神与心灵过程。", "src": "第 531、653、672、789 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 531 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 653 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 672 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 789 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "头脑是个过滤器，信念是筛网", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0034", "zh": "可以称头脑为心智的实质副本。借由头脑，灵魂与智力的功能可以和身体相连，透过头脑的特性，非实质根源的事件转变成了实质有效的事件，那么就会有一个明确过滤和集中的效果在运作。实际来说，你的确透过有意识的信念形成了实相所采取的面貌，那些信念被作为过滤与指挥的原动力，把某一些非实质的“可能事件”由其他区域带入了三度空间来实现。", "en": "(A long pause at 10:06.) The brain can be called simply the physical counterpart of the mind. By means of the brain the functions of the soul and intellect are connected with the body. Through the characteristics of the brain, events that are of nonphysical origin become physically valid. There is a definite filtering and focusing effect at work, then. Practically speaking, you do indeed form the appearance that reality takes through your conscious beliefs. Those beliefs are used as screening and directing agents, separating certain nonphysical probable events from others, and bringing them into three-dimensional actuality.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第653节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "653", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0035", "zh": "其他的可能事件也同样可能会变成你的实质经验。你对自己的那些信念，会形成你的自我形象，而后会对你认为什么是可能或不可能下定义。因此，从那些非实质的可能事件里，你只选出那些能符合你的。", "en": "Other probable events could just as well become physically experienced ones. Those beliefs about yourself form your own self-image, and define your concepts of what is possible or not possible for you. You will choose from those nonphysical probable events, therefore, only those you feel you are in accord with.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第653节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "653", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0036", "zh": "以你们的话来谈，可能的事件被带入现实，是借由身体的神经系统而透过意志或有意识信念的某种强度来达到的。", "en": "In your terms probable events are brought into actuality by utilizing the body’s nerve structure through certain intensities of will or conscious belief.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第653节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "653", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0042", "zh": "潜在地，你的意识是有能力来做这些伟绩的，但这工作不能以你那强烈执着于时空关系的意识来做。你视为“意识心”的那个东西，是被给予估量每日生活“事实”的重任，然后形成对实相的信念，这些信念再被用于梦境而作为主要的尺度之一，可以说，以启动某些可能事件而非其他的。", "en": "Latently, your consciousness is capable of performing these feats, but the work cannot be done with the part of your consciousness that is strongly attached to the space-time relationship. What you think of as your conscious mind is given the task of assessing the “facts” of daily living. It then forms beliefs about reality, and these are used in the dream state as one of the main yardsticks, so to speak, that activate the emergence of certain probable events rather than others.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第672节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "672", "only_en": true}]}, {"label": "落地要满足的条件", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0069", "zh": "为了要变成实质，可能事件必须合于某种条件。它们必须落入恰当的时空位置里。也必须有一种心理上的适合，在欲望、信念或意向上达到某种强度。说到强度，我不一定是指努力、热切的欲望、或下决心的有意识的意向。我是指某种无形质量的集合体，精确地向实质的活动集中焦点。", "en": "In order to become physical, probable events must meet certain conditions, as it were. They must fall into the proper time and space slots. There must be a psychological fit also, certain intensities reached in terms of desire, belief, or intent. By intensity I do not necessarily mean effort, vehement desire, or determined conscious intent. I mean instead the collection of certain intangible qualities, precisely focused toward physical activity.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第789节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "789", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0070", "zh": "实质事件暗示了基本上非实质的力量集合成为一个组织，这组织原本是存在于时空范畴之外的。这是一个心理的组织，包含一组被选择的可能事件。可以这么说，这些在侧翼等待着实质地实现。那实现的最终发动也可来自醒时或梦中状态，但它代表所需的最后因素——灵感、欲望或目的之加快——那将突然地发动原本的心理组织，而成为实质的发生。", "en": "Physical events imply the collection of basically nonphysical forces into an organization that exists initially outside of the time-space context. This is a psychological organization, consisting of a selection of chosen probable events. These wait in the wings, so to speak, for physical actualization. The final trigger for that actualization may come from the waking or dream states, but it will represent the final factor needed — the quickening of inspiration, desire, or purpose — that will suddenly activate the initial psychological organization as a physical occurrence.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第789节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "789", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0075", "zh": "你们每一个人以信念与意图告诉内我，在无量的可能事件中你想遇上哪一个。在梦境，由两个架构来的事件都被处理。梦境不只牵涉到存在于两个实相架构之间的一种意识状态，却还牵涉到——以那种说法——一个连接的实相本身。在此，我想强调所有种类的植物与动物生命都“做梦”。这同样适用于原子分子及任何“粒子”的“心理活动”。", "en": "[Each of] you, with your beliefs and intents, tell the inner ego which of an infinite number of probable events you want to encounter. In the dream state events from both frameworks are processed. The dream state involves not only a state of consciousness that exists between the two frameworks of reality, but also involves, in those terms, a connecting reality of its own. Here I would like to emphasize that to one degree or another all species of plant and animal life “dream.” The same applies to the “psychological activity” of atoms and molecules, and any “particle.”2 Period.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第823节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "823", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0029", "zh": "例如，在一个非常单纯的层面，你的意识常在睡眠状态中离开了身体。你与你认识的其他层面的实相中的人们沟通，但远超过这个，你创造性地维护你的肉体形相并使它复苏。你处置每日的经验，把它投射到你所认为的将来，由无限的可能事件中选择你愿将它具体化的那些，而开始那把它们带进物质世界的精神上与心灵上的过程。", "en": "At a very simple level, for example, your consciousness leaves your body often in the sleep state. You communicate with people in other levels of reality that you have known, but far beyond this, you creatively maintain and revitalize your physical image. You process daily experience, project it into what you think of as the future, choose from an infinity of probable events those you will make physical, and begin the mental and psychic processes that will bring them into the world of substance.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第531节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "531", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B05", "title": "赛斯给的几个画面：星球、火箭船与语言的停顿", "note": "第 789 节干脆让你假装自己是一颗星球：空间里住满各种居民，赛斯就把这些太空居民比作可能的事件；星球有某些特性，有些居民根本没法在那种条件下着陆，而那些条件代表的正是你自己的心理独特性——太空旅客要把自己的火箭船着陆，必须进入你的大气并利用它的条件，同时保持它们自己的完整性，而且它们也必有自己来做这次拜访的理由。同一节紧接着把镜头拉近：任何实质事件都有点像一只火箭船从「别的某处」进入你的世界时的冲击，思想常常仿佛游进游出你的意识系统而你几乎没注意到，事件也以同样方式出现又消失，却影响了你的实相；你在某种程度上吸引了它们、它们也被你所吸引，一个极度充电的关联场暂时被建立起来，提供一条内在通路，让可能事件流进你可认知的事件范围。第 780 节换了个更抽象的画面：任意一件事都暗示了那些没有「浮现」、未被「说出」的可能事件之存在，物质世界的事件因此依赖被暗示的可能事件——他拿语言作比，你的实相里只是停顿的地方，在别的可能性里被强调成事件，别的世界就住在它上面。第 788 节把尺度放到最大：每个意识以自己的方式盖印宇宙，它的存在本身就立起一种重要意义，宇宙借这种意义诠释自己、认识自己；每个意识被赋予多次元的创造力，会以自己那份重要意义为焦点，把宇宙上对它可能的事件全吸进自己的经验，并用这些意义当尺度来接受或排除可能事件。", "src": "第 780、788、789 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 780 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 788 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 789 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "你是星球，事件是来客", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0071", "zh": "让我们用一个比喻。假装你是个星球，就如在某方面来说你的确是的（Pretend that you are a planet, as indeed in certain terms you are.）。你存在于一个极为复杂与成熟的宇宙里。你知道，空间是由各种居民所填满，而我们将把这些太空的居民比喻为「可能的事件」。作为一个星球，你有某些特性。有些太空居民根本不能在那些条件下着陆。这些条件代表你自己心理上的个人性。你对星星们发出讯息，因为你很寂寞，而事件或访客是你获取经验与知识的主要方法之一。为了将自己的火箭船着陆，太空旅客必须进入你的大气而利用它的条件，同时保持它们自己的完整性。它们也必有自己的理由来做这样的一次拜访。", "en": "Let us use an analogy. Pretend that you are a planet, as indeed in certain terms you are. You exist in a highly complicated and sophisticated universe. You know that space is filled with all kinds of inhabitants, and we will compare these space inhabitants to probable events. As a planet you have certain characteristics. Some space inhabitants would not be able to land under those conditions at all. The conditions represent your own psychological individuality. You send out messages to the stars because you are lonely, and events or visitors are one of your main methods of gaining experience and knowledge. To land their own rocket ships, space travelers must enter your atmosphere and use its conditions while maintaining their own integrity. They must also have their own reasons for such a visit.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第789节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "789", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0072", "zh": "且说，任何实质事件就有些像是从「别的某处」进入你世界的一只火箭船的冲击（Now any physical event is something like the impact of a rocket ship entering your world from “somewhere else.”）。思想常常仿佛游进或游出你的意识系统，而你几乎没注意到。事件常以同样的方式出现又消失，但它们却影响了你的实相。你在某种程度上吸引了它们，而它们被你所吸引。一个相关的场暂时被建立了，它是极度充电的。它提供了一个内在通路，经由它，可能事件能流进你可认知的事件的范围。", "en": "Now any physical event is something like the impact of a rocket ship entering your world from “somewhere else.” Thoughts often seem to swim in and out of your system of consciousness, and you barely notice. Events often appear and disappear in the same manner, yet they have impressed your reality. You have attracted them to one extent or another, and they have been attracted to you. Momentarily a field of relatedness is set up that is highly charged, one that provides an inner path by which probable events can flow into your area of recognized events.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第789节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "789", "only_en": true}]}, {"label": "没说出口的那些，撑起你的世界", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0064", "zh": "我说过，所有的事件同时发生——一个难以了解的评论。所有的身份也是同时发生的，每件事改变了其它每一件事。现在的事改变了过去的事。任意一件事暗示了那没有「浮现」的、未被「说出」的可能事件之存在。实质的世界事件因此依赖被暗示的可能事件的存在。不同的语言以它自己奇特的方式利用声音，有它自己的节奏，其强调另一个所忽略的（Different languages use sounds in their own peculiar manners, with their own rhythms, one emphasizing what another ignores.）。因此，其它的可能性强调那些在你们的实相里只被暗示（当作停顿）的事件，因此你们的实质事件改变成了被暗示的可能的事件，而其它的世界依赖其上（Other probabilities, therefore, emphasize events that are only implied (as pauses) in your reality, so that your physical events become the implied probable ones upon which other worlds reside.）。", "en": "I have said that all events occur at once — a difficult statement to understand. All identities occur at once also. Each event changes every other. Present ones alter past ones. Any one event implies the existence of probable events which do not “emerge,” which are not “spoken.” Physical world events therefore rest upon the existence of implied probable events. Different languages use sounds in their own peculiar manners, with their own rhythms, one emphasizing what another ignores. Other probabilities, therefore, emphasize events that are only implied (as pauses) in your reality, so that your physical events become the implied probable ones upon which other worlds reside.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第780节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "780", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0067", "zh": "每个意识也以自己的印记「盖印」或「印在」宇宙上。宇宙没有哪部分是不活动或被动的，不管它看似有组织或缺乏组织。那么，每个意识以自己的方式盖印在宇宙上。它的存在本身就建立了一种重要的意义（significance），宇宙就透过这样的意义被诠释。宇宙透过此种意义而认识自己。每个意识都被赋予了多次元的创造力，因此它将寻求去创造尽可能多的实相，用它自己的重要意义为焦点，从宇宙身上，把所有可能的事件吸进它的经验。于是每个意识从宇宙吸引事件，而它自身的存在同时又盖印在宇宙之上，成为一个带有它天性中不可磨灭的印记的事件。", "en": "It also “stamps” or “impresses” the universe with its own imprint. No portion of the universe is inactive or passive, regardless of its seeming organization or its seeming lack of organization. Each consciousness, then, impresses the universe in its own fashion. Its very existence sets up a kind of significance, in whose light the rest of the universe will be interpreted. The universe knows itself through such significances. Each consciousness is endowed with creativity of a multidimensional nature, so that it will seek to create as many possible realities for itself as it can, using its own significance as a focus to draw into its experience whatever events are possible for it from the universe itself. It will then attract events from the universe, even as its own existence imprints the universe as an event with the indelible stamp of its own nature.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第788节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "788", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0068", "zh": "利用这种重要意义做尺度，你接受或排除可能的事件。你以你自己的重要意义盖在宇宙上，而用那作为一个焦点，从宇宙汲取或吸引那些适合你独特目的及需要的事件。在如此做时，到某程度上你增强了宇宙所能创造出的诸多可能。你由宇宙形成了一个个人的实相，那是非此便不会存在的，以那种说法来说。而且在如此做时，借着增加所有意识从中汲取的实相的库藏，你也不可计量地增加了其它意识的实相。", "en": "Using such significances as yardsticks, you accept or reject probable events. You imprint the universe with your own significance, and using that as a focus you draw from it, or attract, those events that fit your unique purposes and needs. In doing so, to some extent you multiply the creative possibilities of the universe, forming from it a personal reality that would otherwise be absent, in those terms; and in so doing you also add in an immeasurable fashion to the reality of all other consciousness by increasing the bank of reality from which all consciousness draws.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第788节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "788", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B06", "title": "梦是或然事件的工作台", "note": "第 231 节先把门槛拆掉：思想的确是事件，愿望和渴望是事件，它们也影响那些你在物质宇宙里以真实事件感知到的行动，人类系统对这些事件的回应不亚于对物质事件的回应；而在梦的状态，这些可能事件的一部分常以半意识的方式被经验，那等同于渗漏——赛斯说他是有目的性地用这个字。同一节说明谁在做这份工作：潜意识多少熟悉可能性，到某种程度以问题解决的方式来经验它们，它心中以自我的目的检视这些可能性，因而帮助自我决定哪一个可能事件该被选作自己的经验。第 707 节把这条通道讲得更具体：你私人的心灵密切关怀你的俗世存在，在梦境里你与可能的行动打交道，常常在那种状态中想出与一连串可能事件有关的难题解法。第 785 节把身体也拉进来：睡眠里每个细胞计算各种可能事件在它自己实相上的影响，使身体的全部反应能在事前被决定并估量利弊，身体在最细微的层面参与做梦。第 779 节留下这一页最有冲击力的一条判据：心灵不计时间，对它而言你生命中强烈的经验同时存在，所以某些你梦过却没有实现的事件对心灵相当真实，比昨晨早餐那种平淡却确实的物质事件远较真实。第 668 节给出日常后果：你常常在一些情况里表现得卓越，而那是肉身层面的你完全不知觉的；肉身的你只觉察到可能事件的一条线，所以许多梦的事件的意义逃过了你的注意力。第 720 节顺带说破「拍照」这件事：醒时你经验为真实的事件，是一般摄影师唯一抓得到的，而梦世界呈现的事件范畴大得多，所以梦的照相机也会捕捉住可能的事件。第 795 节解释为什么这一切值得：梦循着联想之路突破时间障碍，让人把生命不同时段的事件混合、配对、比较，像小孩玩游戏一样自由地演百万个角色、检查可能事件的本质；人类对事件反应的巨大多样性极依赖这种做梦能力，人在梦里试演自己对可能事件的可能反应，因而对「未来的」行动有较周全的准备。第 933 节把梦说成一项生产力最高的企业：你在梦里玩那些被考虑要具体实现的事件之种种版本，并在个人层面上「看」你的家庭、部落、组织、社区及国家将要实现的可能事件。", "src": "第 231、668、707、720、779、785、795、933 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 231 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 668 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 707 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 720 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 779 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 785 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 795 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 933 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "梦替你试演、替你算题", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0012", "zh": "因此，思想的确是事件。愿望和渴望是事件。就如你所知，愿望和渴望也影响着那些行动，那些在物质宇宙里被你以真实事件感知的行动。人类系统全然地回应着这些事件，就好像它回应着物质事件一样。在梦的状态，这些可能事件的一部分常常会以半意识的方式经验着。这等同于渗漏，而我是有目的性的使用这个字眼，这里可以用你们的录音机来做一个比喻。", "en": "Thoughts are indeed events therefore. Wishes and desires are events. As you know, wishes and desires also influence those actions which will be perceived by you as actual events in the physical universe. The human system responds fully as much to these events as it does to physical events. In the dream state often portions of these probable events are experienced in a semiconscious manner. This amounts to a bleed-through, and I use the term purposely, for your recorder can serve us here in an analogy.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第231节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "231", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0014", "zh": "当这情形发生的时候，它通常是从潜意识里，从梦境渗漏出来，因为潜意识多少是熟悉可能性的，而到某种程度以问题解决的方式来经验这些可能性。它在心中以自我的目的来检视这些可能性，因此帮助自我来决定哪一个可能性事件应该被选为它自己的经验。", "en": "When this does happen it usually occurs as a bleed-through from the subconscious from the dream state, for the subconscious is somewhat acquainted with probabilities, and to some degree experiences these in a problem-solving manner. It views various probabilities with the purposes of the ego in mind, and therefore aids the ego in its decisions as to which probable events it should choose for its own experience.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第231节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "231", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0057", "zh": "现在：你私人的心灵密切的关怀你俗世的存在，而在你的梦境里，你与可能的行动打交道，而常常找出在那个情况里升出的有关一连串可能事件之间的解决之道。", "en": "Now. Your private psyche is intimately concerned with your earthly existence, and in your dream state you deal with probable actions, and often work out in that condition the solutions to problems or questions that arise having to do with probable sequences of events.4 On many occasions then you set yourself a problem — “Shall I do this or that?” — and form a dream in which you follow through the probable futures that would “result” from the courses available. While you are sleeping and dreaming, your chemical and hormonal activity faithfully follows the courses of the dreams. Even in your accepted reality, then, to that extent in such a dream you react to probable events as well as to the events chosen for waking physical experience. Your daily life is affected, because in such a dream you deal with probable predictabilities. You are hardly alone, however, so each individual alive also has his and her private dreams, and these help form the accepted probability sequence of the following day, and of “time to come.” The personal decisions all add up to the global happenings on any given day.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第707节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "707", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0045", "zh": "在梦里，你知悉可能事件，而后你从中选择；（对我：）所以，当你作为一个孩子而死了之前，你知道你可以选择那死亡。广义说来，你选择生与死二者，而你那张十六岁时的照片在那个实相里根本没有拍。", "en": "In dreams you are acquainted with probable events, from which you then choose; (to me:) so before you died as a child, you knew that you could pick or choose that death. In greater terms you chose both life and death, and the picture of you at the age of 169 was never taken in one reality.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第681节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "681", "only_en": true}]}, {"label": "梦里的成果照样算数", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0040", "zh": "你常常在一些情况里表现得很卓越，而那是在肉身层面的你完全不知觉的。虽然如此，这些成就仍然会透过你目前的焦点来运作，既然肉身的你只觉察到可能事件的一条线，因此，许多梦的事件的意义就逃过了你的注意力。但你在梦里做的事，常常与你在白天做的一样有效，而且在梦境里你与“转世的自己”也可以相遇，并相互地影响。", "en": "You often excel in situations that utterly escape you at a physical level. These accomplishments still operate through the focus of your present, since you are physically aware of but one line of probable events, so the meaning of many dream events escapes you. But in dreams you often do work quite as valid as any performed in the day, and in the dream state you meet and interact with your own reincarnational selves.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第668节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "668", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0063", "zh": "你读你自己，从那一页的顶到底，或从你所认为的起头到结尾。可是，你较广大的实相是以其强度的大小来被阅读的，因此心灵以一个不同的方式把你组合起来。心灵不计时间，对它而言，你生命中强烈的经验同时存在。以你的话来说，它们将是心灵的现在。不过，心灵处理可能的事件，因为某些事件——也许有些你梦见但没有实现的——对心灵来说相当的真实。它们对于心灵来说，比大部分平淡却确切的实相事件——如昨晨的早餐——远较真实。", "en": "You read yourselves from the top of the page to the bottom, or from what you think of as the beginning to the end. Your greater reality, however, is read in terms of intensities, so that the psyche puts you together in a different way. The psyche does not mark time. To it the intense experiences of your life exist simultaneously. In your terms they would be the psyche’s present. The psyche deals with probable events, however, so some events — perhaps some that you dreamed of but did not materialize — are quite real to the psyche. They are far more real to it than most innocuous but definite physical events, as for example yesterday morning’s breakfast.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第779节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "779", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0065", "zh": "这些试映不止是为了心智，也是为了身体。再次的，在睡眠里，每个细胞计算各种可能的事件在它自己实相上的影响。细胞做出计算，以使身体的全部反映都能在事前决定，并估量其利弊。在最细微的层面，身体参与了梦。", "en": "Now these previews are played out not only for the mind but for the body as well. In sleep, again, each cell calculates the effect of various probable events upon its own reality. Computations are made so that the body’s entire response can be ascertained ahead of time, and the advantages and disadvantages weighed. The body participates in dreaming at the most minute levels.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第785节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "785", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0059", "zh": "在醒时你体验某些事件为真实的，一般而言，这些是可以被一个普通的摄影师所抓住的唯一事件。可是，梦世界呈现了一个大得多的事件之范畴，许多事件后来会显出为具体的事件，同时，其它一样有效的事件则否。所以，梦照相机也会捕捉住可能的事件。", "en": "In waking life you experience certain events as real, and generally these are the only ones that can be captured by an ordinary photographer. The dream world,1 however, presents a much larger category of events. Many [events] may later appear as physical ones, while others just as valid will not. The dream camera, therefore, will capture probable events also.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第720节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "720", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "像玩游戏一样检查可能性", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0073", "zh": "因为梦循着联想之路，它们突破了时间的障碍，容许个人将他生命不同时段的事件混合、配对和比较。所有这些多少是以儿童游戏的方式去做。透过创造性的梦的戏剧之形成，在其中个人从“一个游戏”的立足点，可自由地去演百万个不同的角色，去检查可能事件的本质。", "en": "Because dreams follow paths of association, they break through time barriers, allowing the individual to mix, match, and compare events from different periods of his life. All of this is done somewhat in the way that a child plays, through the formation of creative dream dramas in which the individual is free to play a million different roles and to examine the nature of probable events from the standpoint of “a game.”", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第795节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "795", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0074", "zh": "人类对事件反应的伟大多样性，极为依赖这种做梦的能力。在梦中，人类尝试他对可能事件的可能反应，因而对“未来的”行动有较周全的准备。", "en": "The great versatility of the species in its reaction to events is highly dependent upon this kind of dreaming capacity. The species tries out its probable reactions to probable events in the dream state, and hence is better prepared for action “in the future.”", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第795节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "795", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0079", "zh": "所以，做梦的主要目的之一，就是增进人的快乐，那是指增进生活的品质本身。梦是综合了精神活动与游戏、心灵与情感的丰富创造性戏剧。当你在梦里开始去玩那些被考虑要具体实现的事件之种种版本，当你在梦里在一个个人层面上“看”你的家庭、部落、组织、社区及国家将实现的可能事件时，梦也将你卷入最具生产力的企业里。", "en": "(Long pause at 9:00.) One of the main purposes of dreaming, therefore, is to increase man’s pleasure, which means to increase the quality of living itself. Dreams are mental work and play combined, psychic and emotional rich creative dramas. They also involve you in the most productive of enterprises as you begin to play with versions of events that are being considered for physical actualization, as on a personal level you “view” the probable events which your family, tribe, organization, community and country will actualize.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第933节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "933", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B07", "title": "预知为什么会算错", "note": "第 234 节回答新手最容易撞上的那个问题：预知看到的为什么常跟后来不一样。赛斯说，有些情形是因为一个自己选择了一个不同的可能事件来物质化；他讲自己可以进入可能性的场域而你们不行（以自我而言不行），你们的过去、现在与未来在他那里汇集成同一个。同节把这条推远：未来也在改变，你以改变过去的方式同样改变着未来；这种情形里必须感知到那条正确的可能事件频道，正确指的是最终会被选择去实化的那条，而这个选择同时凭借你在过去和现在的选择。第 679 节补上机制：情况虽然在一个方式上已经被「设定」，在另一个方式上却高度可塑，因此各式各样的可能事件能从它们流出来；预知性地说，你对任何一个行为或路线的结果在无意识上都十分觉察。第 493 节罕见地把这条用在具体的人身上：赛斯说当鲁柏感知到那些印象时诸可能性指向一场事故会发生，但基于他前面给过的理由，这件事如今已远不那么可能了；紧接着那句是这一页里最有行动力的——把这份信息告诉你的朋友，还会使它进一步不那么可能发生。第 437 节给了可以上手的练法：就像你正要开始画一幅画那样，请求看见某些人在未来某个时间或某种情境里的影像，然后让你的能力在想象的图样上自行补足。第 741 节收尾：当你试着在一些可能事件里决定哪一个会具体发生，你就越来越在与可能性打交道；当可能性的问题变成一个实际问题，科学家们就会给它更多考虑。", "src": "第 234、437、493、679、741 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 234 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 437 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 493 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 679 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 741 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "预言落空的真正原因", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0024", "zh": "现在。预知的讯息常常看起来是错的。在一些例子里，这是因为自己选择了一个不同的可能性事件物质化。我可以进入可能性的场域而你们不行——自我上，你们不行。我可以进入你们所谓的过去。我能看到你们的现在。对我来说，你们的过去、现在和未来合并成同一个。", "en": "Now. Often clairvoyant information will appear to be wrong. In some cases this is because a different probable event has been chosen by a self for physical materialization. I have access to the field of probabilities and you do not—egotistically, you do not. I have access to your so-called past. I can view your present. To me your past, present and future merge into one.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第234节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "234", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0025", "zh": "不过在大部分的例子，你们所感兴趣的是未来。未来也在改变。你以改变过去的方式同样改变着未来。在这样的例子，可能性事件那个正确路线的被感知是必要的，正确意味着最终被选择的路线。而这个选择是同时凭借着你过去和现在的选择。", "en": "In most cases however it is the future in which you are interested. The future changes. You change it, in the same manner that you have changed the past. In such cases it is necessary that the correct channel of probable events be perceived, correct meaning the channel which shall ultimately be chosen. And the choice is dependent upon your choices in both past and present.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第234节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "234", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0044", "zh": "不过，那些情况虽然在一种方式里被“设定”了，但在另一种方式却是非常具可塑性的，因此，各式各样的可能事件能自它们流出。预知性地说，你对任何一个行为或路线之结果在无意识上都十分的觉察。当鲁柏这张照片被拍下时，他已开始变得觉察到那些会主宰他未来生活的他全盘兴趣之所在，虽然其特定路线尚未被选择。", "en": "The conditions, however, while “set” in one fashion, are highly plastic in another, so that a multitudinous variety of probable events can flow from them. Precognitively you are unconsciously quite aware — again in your terms — of the results of any given action or cause. When this (photograph) of Ruburt3 was taken, he had already become aware of the overall interests and concerns that would dominate his future life, although the particular course of it had not been chosen.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第679节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "679", "only_en": true}]}, {"label": "把可能性谈给活人：练法与实例", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0026", "zh": "我想，几次尝试之后，你应该能够——可以说——调到某些未来的可能事件上，以影像的形式接收。做的时候，就好像你正要开始画一幅画，请求看见某些人在未来某个时间或某种情境里的影像，然后让你的能力在想象的图样上自行补足。这个做法应该很适合你。", "en": "You should be able to tune in, so to speak, on some future probable events, I believe in image form, after a few attempts. Do this as if you were starting out to paint a picture, requesting to see the images of certain persons at a given time or situation in the future, and let your abilities then fill in on the imaginative pattern. This procedure should suit you well.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第437节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "437", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0027", "zh": "我们那位耶稣会士会想伤害自己，因而成为这类事故中的受害者，或者成为某种独自一人之事故的受害者。那两个少年则会想伤害别人。不过，无数要素此刻仍能介入，改变他们的可能性；只是，当鲁柏感知到那些印象时，诸可能性指向的便是这件事会发生。基于前述理由，这件事如今已远不那么可能发生。", "en": "Our Jesuit would want to hurt himself, and become therefore the victim in such an accident, or be the victim of some kind of one-man affair. The boys would want to hurt others. Unnumbered elements can still intrude to change their probability, even now, however; but the probabilities when Ruburt perceived the impressions were that the event would occur. For the reasons given this is now far less a probable events.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第493节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "493", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0028", "zh": "把这份信息告诉你们的朋友，还会使它进一步不那么可能发生。你们明白我的意思吗？", "en": "Giving this information to your friends will make it still less a probable events. Do you follow me?", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第493节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "493", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0062", "zh": "当你试着决定在一些可能事件里哪一个会具体发生时，你就开始越来越与可能性打交道了。当可能的问题是一个实际的问题时，那么，科学家们就会给它更多的考虑了。", "en": "More and more, you are beginning to deal with probabilities as you try to ascertain which of a number of probable events might physically occur. When the question of probabilities is a practical one, then scientists will give it more consideration.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第741节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "741", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B08", "title": "落到你自己身上：一桩入室盗窃，和一件可以换掉的往事", "note": "第 656 节举了一个谁都懂的例子：今天你家被偷了，而在昨天，这桩偷窃只是无数的可能事件之一；赛斯选它是因为里面涉及不止一个人——受害者与小偷。为什么被洗劫的是你家而不是你邻居的家？他的答案是：在一个方式或另一个方式里，你透过有意识的思想吸引了这样一个事件，把它由可能性带入事实，这件事会是一股能量的聚集，被随之而来的信念造成。第 657 节把范围摊开：同样的原则可以用在你生活的任何领域，在每一处你都是从各种可能的事件里选择——但这种效果只有当你停止从过去寻找「毛病何在」、停止加强你的负面经验时才会发生。第 566 节给出最反直觉的用法：想象那件非常困扰你的事不只是被抹去，而是被一件本质上更有益的事件取代，要非常栩栩如生、在情感上有确实的感受、反复观想；赛斯说这并非自欺，你选择去想象的事件自动就会是一个可能的事件，它确实发生过，只是不是你在你「可能的过去」里选择感知的那个。第 675 节交代代价与前提：要利用将来的可能事件，实质的大脑将被迫扩大它的作用，同时使个人与当下的威力之点保持清楚的关系；他顺手把「肯定」定义为你当下力量的承认，「否定」就是对那力量的放弃。第 695 节留了一个当天就能做的练习：拿你读这一页那天发生在你身上的任何一件事，把那特定被选的事件看作是从其他可能会发生的事件之庞大库藏里来到你经验里的一个。", "src": "第 566、653、656、657、675、695 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 566 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 653 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 656 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 657 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 675 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 695 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "昨天它还只是无数可能之一", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0033", "zh": "继续题目：（你日常实相是特定可能事件的表现）那就是全部的题目。", "en": "Continuing with the heading: “Your Daily Reality as the Expression of Specific Probable Events.” That is all the heading.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第653节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "653", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0038", "zh": "在个人和集体的方式下都是这样运作的。假设今天你家被偷了，然而在昨天，这个偷窃只是无数的可能事件之一而已。我选择这个例子是因为涉及了不止一个人——受害者与小偷。（停顿。）为什么你的家会被洗劫，而不是你邻居的家呢？无论如何，透过有意识的思想，你吸引这样一个事件，而把它由可能性带入了事实。这件事将是一个能量的聚集——转变成一个行动——而被自然造成的信念带来。", "en": "This operates in individual and mass terms. Suppose that today your home was robbed. Yesterday, the theft was one of innumerable probable events. I chose such an example because more than one person would have to be involved — the victim and the robber. (Pause.) Why was your home ransacked, and not your neighbor’s? In one way or another, through your conscious thought you attracted such an event, and drew it from probability into actuality. The occurrence would be an accumulation of energy — turned into action — and be brought about by corollary beliefs.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第656节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "656", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0039", "zh": "然而，只有当你停止从过去寻找“毛病何在”，停止加强你的负面经验时，这种效果才会发生。这些同样的原则可以被用在你生活的任何领域，在每个里面，你都是从各种可能的事件里选择。", "en": "Such effects will occur however only if you cease looking into the past “for what is wrong,” and stop reinforcing your negative experience. These same principles can be used in any area of your life, and in each you are choosing from a variety of probable events.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第657节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "657", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "改写往事与当天的练习", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0030", "zh": "假设发生了某一特定事件令你非常困扰。那么你就在心中想象着，不仅它被抹去了，并且以一件本质上更有益的事件来取代之。现在这个想象必须要非常的栩栩如生，在情感上有确实的感受，而且要反复的观想。这并非自欺。你所选择去想象的事件会自动地是个可能的事件，也确实地发生了，虽然不是你在你“可能的过去”所选择感知的那个事件。", "en": "Pretend a particular event happened that greatly disturbed you. In your mind imagine it not simply wiped out, but replaced by another event of more beneficial nature. Now this must be done with great vividness and emotional validity, and many times. It is not a self-deception. The event that you choose will automatically be a probable event, which did in fact happen, though it is not the event you chose to perceive in your given probable past.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第566节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "566", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0043", "zh": "大脑必须整理这些资讯，以使向肉体对准的机制能够清楚地维持它时间上的“现在”。当人类第一次发展了一个“反省”的暂停，如在这本书里早先提及的（在第九章第六三五到六三六节），在他学会去区分一个生动地忆起的过去事件，与一个目前在经验的事件之前，他的确要经过最初的“方向迷失感”。成长中的意识必须为实际行为来做这种区分。去利用将来的可能事件，实质的大脑将被迫扩大它的作用，同时使个人与当下的威力之点或现世的效力保持清楚的关系。“肯定”永远涉及了对你当下力量的承认。广义而言，“否定”就是对那个力量的放弃。那么，“肯定”就是默认你——作为在肉体内的心灵——去形成动物性物质实相的能力。", "en": "Now the brain would have to sort out this information so that the physically attuned mechanism was clearly able to maintain its temporal present. When man first developed the pause of reflection, as mentioned earlier in this book (see sessions 635–36 in Chapter Nine), he did undergo initial disorientation before he learned to distinguish a vividly remembered event of the past from a presently experienced one. The growing consciousness had to make such distinctions for practical behavior. To utilize future probable events, the physical brain would be forced to enlarge its function while keeping the individual in clear relationship with the present moment of power, or corporeal effectiveness. Affirmation always involves the acknowledgement of your power in the present. In greater terms, denial is the surrendering of that power. Affirmation then is the acquiescence to your ability, as a spirit within flesh, to form the physical reality of your creaturehood.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第675节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "675", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0052", "zh": "练习单元2 我希望每个读者试试两个练习。首先，拿一件你读这篇东西的那天发生在你身上的任何事，把那特定被选的事件看作是由其他可能会发生的事件之庞大库藏中来到你经验里的一个。", "en": "I would like each reader to try two exercises. First of all, take any incident that happens to you the day you read this page. See the particular chosen event as one that came into your experience from the vast bank of other probable events that could have occurred.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第695节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "695", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B09", "title": "集体那一层：文化、历史与别人记忆里的版本", "note": "第 695 节先把这一页收在你自己身上：你所认为的日常生活，是一种对某些可能事件而非其他的聚焦，一种对重要意义的选择、一种对模式的抉择，而自己的其他部分则跟随了不同的抉择。第 694 节解释这一串是怎么凝成形的：可能事件经常在相互作用，透过它们的互动，你终于得到一串为你们所接受、被认知的事件，称之为物质实相——那里确实有一个组织存在着，只是不是你习于认知的那种秩序。同节另一段把尺度放大：你切身的每日实相可以被可能性涉及并染色，被你自己的欲望及信念带入经验；同样，你们群体的文化、世界的历史以及人类的取向，也被那些并不切合你们对物质实相之官方认可概念的可能事件所染色。第 697 节说明这种筛选为什么必要：任何系统里的理想化都已经把某些种类的事件接受为重要的，而把其他同样可能的事件剔除为不具重要意义，这只不过提供了一个成就与经验可以在其中发生的可行焦点。第 729 节处理两个人对同一件事记忆不同的难题：你们集中焦点在经验的相似处、减低不同处的重要性，于是更大的不相似处完全逃过注意；你以为诠释会变但某些明确的事件不可改变，赛斯说事件本身其实并没那么扎实——你接受一个可能事件，别人体验到的是那事件的另一个版本，那就变成他所感受的实相。第 728 节更彻底：时间由其内部增殖，同样生在一九四〇年但季节与你不同的人，生在一个具有它自己可能事件的不同的一九四〇年。第 588 节给出一段历史性的说明：一个宗教剧终于浮露为外在的代表，但也有许多次要的戏剧、那些「投射」没有全然被采用，这些就代表了可能的事件，它们之中任何一个都可以取代那实际的外在戏剧。第 931 节把这整套当成引擎：事件的内核正是被那种活动维持住的，过去与未来在各方面为你提供一个可能事件的无尽来源，你由之组合你生活及社会的事件。《健康之道》第 8 节把同一条用在跨生上：不是这一生引起另一生，而是有一个可能事件的储存所，每个个人在每一生选择适合自己整体私人目的的那些——一次生命的重病，可能在另一生变成一个不舒服的梦魇，在另一生你可能决定作个医生，为同一个病去找原因和疗法。第 857 节最后把这一切说成宇宙的动力：宇宙及其中每个粒子、波或人的动机力量，是朝向创造的可能性之壮丽动力，以及存在于可能选择与可能事件「之间」的那种充满活力的张力；第 905 节补上它的用处——这一切都与那些区域密切相连，在其中自由意志可以被自由地利用，把可能的事件变成具体被感知的事件。", "src": "第 588、694、695、697、728、729、857、905、931 节；《健康之道》第 8 节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 588 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 694 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 695 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 697 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 728 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 729 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 857 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 905 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 931 节"}, {"n": 10, "id": null, "label": "第 8 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "被接受的现实是互动出来的", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0049", "zh": "只有在一个可能性的范畴里，不朽才有任何的意义。遗传是来自了不起的、天生具有的不可预测性，它然后才被分裂成在染色体内的细目，它们中没有两个是相同的。那么，你所认为的日常生活，是一种对某些可能事件而非其他的聚焦，一种对重要意义的选择，一种对模式的抉择。自己的其他部分则跟随了不同的抉择。", "en": "Only in a context of probabilities can immortality make any sense. Heredity springs from the great inherent unpredictability that is then broken down to specifications inside the chromosomes,9 no two of which are alike. What you think of as daily life is then a focus upon certain probable events above others, a choosing of significances, a selection of pattern. Other portions of the self follow different selections.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第682节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "682", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0050", "zh": "刚才给的比较不重要的可能事件之例子及其相互作用，对可能性的一般性质提供了几个重要的线索。的确是有一个组织存在着，但它却非你们习于认知的那种秩序。在日常生活的所有范围里，这小小的私人经验以不同的变奏被不断的重复着——那是说，可能事件经常在相互作用，而（热切的）透过它们的互动，你们终于有了为你们所接受的、被认知的一串事件，称之为物质实相。", "en": "The relatively insignificant example of probable events and their interaction just given (in the last session) provides a few important clues to the nature of probabilities in general. An organization is definitely present, but it is not the kind of order you are used to recognizing. This small private experience is repeated endlessly with different variations in all areas of daily living — that is, probable events constantly interact, and (intently) through their interaction you end up with one recognized series of episodes that you accept, called physical reality.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第694节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "694", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0051", "zh": "这个插曲也反映了他的信念，因为照他的想法，他将必须舍弃某些自由，而他并没有准备好去这样做。这些事件基本上同时存在，虽然在你们的层面，你们必须在时间里知觉它们。 正如你切身的每日实相可以被可能性涉及并染色，被你自己的欲望及信念带入你的经验里一样，你们群体的文化、世界的历史以及人类的取向也被可能事件所染色，那些是并不切合你们对物质实相之官方认可的概念之事件。", "en": "The episode also mirrored his beliefs, for to his way of thinking he would have to relinquish certain freedoms, and this he was not ready to do. The events basically exist at once, though at your level you have to perceive them in time. As your intimate daily reality can be involved with and colored by probabilities, brought into your experience by your own desires and beliefs, so is your mass culture, world history, and species orientation colored by probable events that do not fit into your officially recognized idea of physical reality.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第694节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "694", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0055", "zh": "在任何系统里理想化已经接受某种事件为重要的，而也已经把其他（也同样可能的事件）剔除为不具重要意义的。这只不过提供了一个成就与经验可以在其中发生的可行焦点。", "en": "In any system the idealization has already accepted certain kinds of events as significant, and has rejected other (quite-as-probable events) as nonsignificant. This simply provides a workable focus in which achievement and experience can happen.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第697节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "697", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "同一年、同一件事，不同的版本", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0060", "zh": "时间由其内部增殖。当你以转世的说法来想，你仍然在处理非常简单的时间观念。假设你生在一九四〇年而你接受一个特定的历史顺序：然而其它在一九四〇出生（在一个与你不同的季节里）的人却是生在一个不同的历史范畴里，一个具有它自己可能事件的一个不同的一九四〇年。你一直把转生想作是被生回到一个你曾读过的历史里。但任何既定的年都有它自己的变奏。", "en": "Time multiplies from within itself. When you think in terms of reincarnation, you are still dealing with very simple time concepts. You accept, if you were born in 1940, a particular historical sequence: but others born in 1940 (in a different season than your own), are born into a different historical context, a different 1940, with its own probable events. You always think of being reincarnated in terms of being born backward into a history of which you have read. But any given year has its own variations.2", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第728节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "728", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0061", "zh": "因为你们集中焦点在经验的相似处，而减低不同处的重要性，于是，常常在所谓经验里的更大不相似处完全逃过了你的注意。如果你对在某个时间与地点发生了什么事与另一个人意见不同的话，你就认为一定是记忆有错误——好比说，那些在最近被经历到的历史事件。你认为当然，对事件的诠释会变，但某些明确的事件发生了，而那是不可改变的。反之，事件本身其实并没那么扎实，你接受一个可能事件，反之，别的人可能体验到那事件的另一个版本，然后，那就变成那个人所感受的实相。", "en": "Because you focus upon the similarities in experience, and play down the variances, then the oftentimes greater dissimilarities4 in so-called experience escape you completely. You take it for granted that memory is faulty if you do not agree with another person on the events that happened at a certain place and time — say those in a recently experienced historical past. You take it for granted that interpretations of events change, but that certain definite events occurred that are beyond alteration. Instead, the events themselves are not nearly that concrete. You accept one probable event. Someone else may experience instead a version of that event, which then becomes that individual’s felt reality.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第729节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "729", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0032", "zh": "在任何一个特定的历史时期，一个宗教剧可能终于浮露出来作为外在的代表，但也有许多次要的戏剧——“投射”——没有全然被采用。自然，这些代表了可能的事件。它们之中的任何一个都可以取代那实际的外在戏剧。在基督的时代有许多这种演出，因为许多人感到内在实相的力量而对之反应。", "en": "In any given historical period, one religious drama may finally emerge as the exterior representation, but there will also be many minor dramas, “projections,” that do not entirely take. These represent, of course, probable events. Any of them could supersede the actual exterior drama. In the time of Christ there were many such performances, as many personalities felt the force of inner reality and reacted to it.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第588节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "588", "only_en": false}]}, {"label": "社会的、跨生的库存", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0078", "zh": "不过，事件的内核正是被那种活动维持住的。你们在各方面都由过去与未来提供了一个可能事件无尽的来源，而由之组合你们生活及社会的事件。再次的，让我提醒你们，所有的时间都同时存在。", "en": "The inner core of events, however, is held together by just that kind of activity. You are at every hand provided an unending source of probable events from past and future, from which to compose the events of your lives and society. Again, let me remind you that all time exists simultaneously.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第931节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "931", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0080", "zh": "我并不是说，在一生里的事件会引起另一生的事件，却是说，有一个整体的模式——一个可能事件的储存所——而在每一生中，每个个人选择适合他整体私人目的的那些。然而那些生生世世将是相连的。一个个人在一次生命中可能有一个严重的疾病。另一生中，那事件可能变成一个不舒服的梦魇。在另一生中，那个人可能有个好友患上同样的病。又在另一生中，那个人可能决定作个医生，给同样的病找到一个原因及一个疗法。", "en": "I am not saying that the events in one life cause the events in another, but that there is an overall pattern — a bank of probable events — and that in each life each individual chooses those that suit his or her overall private purposes. Yet those lives will be connected. An individual may have a serious illness in one life. That event may turn up as one uncomfortable nightmare in another existence. In still another life, the individual might have a dear friend who suffers from the same disease. In still another existence the individual might decide to be a doctor, to seek a cause and a cure for the same disease.", "source": "健康之道 · 第8节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "8", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0076", "zh": "宇宙及在其内的每个粒子、波或人的动机力量，是朝向创造的可能性之壮丽动力，以及存在于可能选择与可能事件“之间”的张力——充满活力的张力。这适用于人及分子以及那些科学家们喜欢用来令他们自己惊奇的假设性推理出来的更小部分——部分或单位。", "en": "The motive power of the universe and of each particle or wave or person within it is the magnificent thrust toward creative probabilities, and the tension that exists, the exuberant tension, that exists “between” probable choices and probable events. This applies to men and molecules, and to all of those hypothetically theorized smaller divisions with which scientists like to amaze themselves. Divisions or units.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第857节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "857", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0077", "zh": "我们也在与可能性打交道，而那资讯与你最终接受为具体经验的那些资料有关：你为什么接受它，它来自何处，以及你没有经验到的那些事件“跑到”哪儿去了。所有这些资料都关联到任何个人从属于整个族类生物的库存，以及从内在转世的库存收到的基因讯息。我们将确保鲁柏会收到他在必要的层面所需的资讯，以使那资料可被口语化。所有这些也都与那些区域密切相连，在其中，自由意志可以被自由的利用，以将可能的事件变成具体被感知的事件。", "en": "We are also dealing with probabilities, and the information has to do with those data you finally accept as physical experience, why you accept it, where it comes from, and where those events “go” that you do not experience. All of this is connected with the genetic information that any individual receives from the biological bank that belongs to the species at large, and from the inner reincarnational bank.1 We will see that Ruburt receives the information that he needs at the necessary levels, so that the material can be verbalized. All of this is also intimately connected with those areas in which free will can be utilized, freely, to turn probable events into physically perceived ones.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第905节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "905", "only_en": false}]}]}], "reading": [{"id": "q-concept-105-e0018", "zh": "可能性的系统并不如那些存在于梦境中的事件那么容易被感知到，因为当你在梦的状态，这是你比较熟悉的。通常自我更觉知到梦的事件。现在那个自己，你们梦里的那个我，可以十分合理的相比于那个经验着可能性事件的自己。", "en": "The system of probabilities is not as easily perceived even as those events that exist within the dream state, for with these you are familiar, while you are in the dream state. Often the ego is even made aware of dream events. Now the self that is the I of your dreams can quite legitimately be compared to the self that experiences probable events.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第232节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "232", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0019", "zh": "现在通过催眠它可以被探知，这个做梦的我有着它过去存在的记忆，是由过去的梦所组成的。如果你喜欢的话，你可以称这个是梦中人格，但它等同于另一个自己。现在，没有选择在物质宇宙里经验的，这个经验着可能性事件的我，有着和这个梦的自己同样的身份和记忆。在自己的这些部分之间，有着持续的潜意识讯息的交换。", "en": "Now through hypnosis it has been ascertained that this dreaming I has memory of its past existence, which is made up of past dreams. You may call this the dreaming personality if you prefer, but it amounts to another self. Now the I who experiences probable events, not chosen for experience in the physical universe, has the same kind of identity and memory as this dream self. There is a constant subconscious interchange of information between these portions of the self.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第232节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "232", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0020", "zh": "现在。我们确实应该在鲁柏不预期的情况下，及时讨论他的梦。现在我想要把一些关于倒置时间和可能性事件的内容加进我们的讨论里。", "en": "Now. We shall indeed discuss Ruburts dream in good time, when he is not expecting it. For now, I would like to add somewhat to our discussion concerning inverted time and probable events.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第233节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "233", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0021", "zh": "可能性的自己和梦的自己之间有一个持续的施与受，这个梦的人格特别从可能性的自己那里收到很多讯息，或这个自己经验了自我称为可能性的事件。", "en": "There is a constant give and take between the probable self and the dreaming self, for much data is received, particularly by the dreaming personality from the probable self, or the self that experiences what the ego would term probable events.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第233节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "233", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0022", "zh": "以这样的说法它本身并不做这些判断。至于决定一个可能的事件是否被感知为一个物质的事件，当然是依赖着随后经验它的自我本质。可能性的自己不做这个决定，而只是把透过它对这个事情的经验资料传过去。", "en": "Of itself however it does not make these judgments in those terms. The decision as to whether or not a particular probable event should be perceived as a physical one depends, of course, upon the nature of the ego which would then experience it. The probable self does not make the decision, but merely passes on the data which it has received through its own experience with the event.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第233节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "233", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0053", "zh": "练习单元4 现在：拿另一张与刚才那张不同年龄的照片，只简单的问你自己：“我是在看同样一个人吗？”这第二张照片有多熟悉或多陌生？它与你今晚选的第一张有何不同？有什么相似性？把这两张照片在你脑海里联合起来，在每张照片被拍下时你有什么样的经验？在一张照片里你想到要跟随，而在那另一张里没被跟随的有那些路子？那些路子的确是被追随的，如果它们没被你所认知的自己追随的话，那么它们也的确被一个以你们的话来说可能的自己追随。当你想到这种事件时，在你的脑子里跟随着那个自己可能会采取的方向，如果你找到一条你现在希望你曾追随但却未追随的发展路线的话，那么深深的去想，那些活动现在可能锲入你官方地被接受的生活之架构的方式。这种带着欲望的沉思——由常识支撑着，能带来在可能性里的交会点，那可引起心灵深层成分的一个新鲜的重新排列。以这种方式，可能事件可以被吸引到你目前的生活结构里。", "en": "Now: Take another photograph of yourself at a different age than the first one you chose. Ask yourself simply: “Am I looking at the same person?” How familiar or how strange is this second photograph? How does it differ from the first one you picked this evening? What similarities are there that unite both photographs in your mind? What experiences did you have when each photograph was taken? What ways did you think of following in one picture that were not followed in the other one? Those directions were pursued. If they were not pursued by the self you recognize, then they were by a self that is probable in your terms. In your mind follow what directions that self would have taken, as you think of such events. If you find a line of development that you now wish you had pursued, but had not, then think deeply about the ways in which those activities could now fit into the framework of your officially accepted life.2 Such musings, with desire — backed up by common sense — can bring about intersection points in probabilities that cause a fresh realignment of the deep elements of the psyche. In such ways probable events can be attracted to your current living structure.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第695节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "695", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-105-e0054", "zh": "你已经摸不到在照片里的那个人了，你是的那个你，在你的经验里可以做任何你想要的改变，你可以为你自己的目的改变可能性，但你却无法改变已走上自己的路的其他可能自己之路线。所有的可能自己是相连的，他们每个彼此影响，有一个自然的相互作用，但却没有强迫。每个可能的自己有他自己的自由意志及独特性，你可以改变在你所知的可能性里你自己的经验——那经验本身就骑在无穷尽的其他可能性上。你可以把任何数目的可能事件带进你自己的经验里，但你却无法改变你实相的另一个部分之可能经验，那是说，你无法消灭它。", "en": "The person within the photograph is beyond your reach. The you that you are can make any changes you want to in your experience: You can change probabilities for your own purposes, but you cannot change the courses of other probable selves that have gone their own ways. All probable selves are connected. They each influence one another. There is a natural interaction, but no coercion. Each probable self has its own free will and uniqueness. You can change your own experience in the probability you know — which itself rides upon infinite other probabilities. You can bring into your own experience any number of probable events, but you cannot deny the probable experience of another portion of your reality. That is, you cannot annihilate it.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第695节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "695", "only_en": true}], "books": [{"name": "早期课第5册", "count": 25}, {"name": "心灵的本质", "count": 12}, {"name": "个人实相的本质", "count": 11}, {"name": "未知的实相卷一", "count": 10}, {"name": "未知的实相卷二", "count": 5}, {"name": "灵魂永生", "count": 4}, {"name": "梦、进化与价值完成", "count": 3}, {"name": "早期课第9册", "count": 3}, {"name": "个人与群体事件的本质", "count": 2}, {"name": "健康之道", "count": 1}], "span": [1966, 1984], "stats": {"blocks": 9, "quotes": 76, "reading": 7, "reading_tagged": 0, "tagged": 0, "only_en": 53}};
